Writing FFI code

CPebble provides a simple FFI for interacting with Pebble code and the runtime using the C programming language. 
This is known as the CPebble Standard C FFI. Please note this FFI differs heavily from the older FFI used in
the Zig implementation of Pebble. This guide is for writing C code using the CPebble C FFI, nothing more.

The CPebble FFI defines two standards, one being used internally by the VM itself, and one being exposed
externally to applications using the FFI. Only the second will be described in this document.

FFI code should always include the file ffi.h; This is used to define the core FFI components needed to use the
FFI itself. This should at least include the structures FFIValue, FFIvars and FFIArena. All of these besides
FFIValue are not to be known to the FFI code, but instead just passed around to helper function defined by ffi.h.
The structure FFIValue should look something like

	typedef enum {
		type_word,
		type_sword,
		type_float,
		type_str,
		type_expr,
	} FFIType;

	typedef struct {
		FFIType Type;
		union {
			unsigned long word;
			long sword;
			double flt;
			char *str;
			ExprNode *expr;
		} as;
	}

Structures FFIType may not be defined under that name, but should have those values in it. The type expr is
opaque and should not be known by the FFI code, but is just some representation of a VM expression tree. This
is also just to be passed around to helper functions.

As a piece of FFI code, I could do something like

	FFIValue v = (FFIValue){ .Type = type_word, .as.word = 42 };

to create a CPebble value who contains the word type 42. FFI code may allocate variables using the interface
FFIallocateVariable, which should be defined as

	void *FFIallocateVariable (FFIvars *vars, const char *varname, void *value, FFIArena *persistAlloc);

where vars is a pointer to the opaque variable table object given to the FFI code, value is a pointer to the
FFIValue defined previously, which in most cases should be allocated using persistAlloc, varname is the name
of the Pebble variable to store it under, and persistAlloc is a pointer to the opaque object representing
one of the FFI allocators.

FFIArena is an opaque structure representing an arena model allocator. Three of these are given to FFI code, being
scratcAlloc, which may be cleared in-between function calls, tempAlloc, which may be cleared in between the
instruction boundary (it is important to note one FFI call counts as one instruction in CPebble), and persistAlloc,
which lasts until the VM dies. These can be used via the FFIallocateMemory helper, which should be defined as

	void *FFIallocateMemory (FFIArena *arena, unsigned long size);

where arena is a pointer to the opaque FFI allocator object and size is the size of the region to allocate. Freeing
memory allocated by FFI code is done by the VM itself, so FFI code does not have to worry about this.

FFI code should also be given a pointer to the VM limits structure, a structure that defines several limits to
certain operations. These should be respected by FFI code, otherwise it is considered malicious usage. This
structure can be viewed by reading h/limits.h, and will not be defined here. Please go read that.

Yes, this does imply FFI code should include limits.h as well.

FFI code is called expected to be

	void some_sequence_using_the_ffi (FFIvars *vars, unsigned long long seed, FFIArena *scratchAlloc, 
		FFIArena *tempAlloc, FFIArena *persistAlloc, Limits *limits);

The name of the FFI sequence (known as an Escape Sequence) should follow the registered name that is exposed
to Pebble. This naming convention exposed to Pebble follows (group).(subgroup(s)).(name), and the equivalent
C function should add escape_ to the start and replace the . character with the _ character. An example could
be the Pebble FFI sequence

	std.io.print

should be defined as the C function

	void escape_std_io_print (FFIvars *vars, unsigned long long seed, FFIArena *scratchAlloc, 
		FFIArena *tempAlloc, FFIArena *persistAlloc, Limits *limits);

The seed variable is a random 64bit seed provided by the virtual machine, which is unique to every VM instance
and FFI sequence. This means if I was to call the same FFI sequence twice within the same VM lifetime, they
would both be given the same random seed. Please keep in mind this seed is not guaranteed to have a full 64bits
of entropy, nor is it to be used for cryptographic purposes.

Arguments given to FFI code are simply stored as normal Pebble variables, which can be read using the
FFIreadVariable and FFIreadVariableUnsafe interfaces. FFIreadVariable will error and fault if the variable does
not exist, while FFIreadVariableUnsafe will return a NULL pointer. These should be defined as

	void *FFIreadVariable (FFIvars *vars, const char *varname);
	void *FFIreadVariableUnsafe (FFIvars *vars, const char *varname);

It is tradition to give FFI arguments under the names of __Escape_, followed by the Pebble Escape sequence name,
followed by _Argon, where n is the argument number, starting at 0 being the first argument. These should contain
pointers to Pebble variables that contain the real argument data. Pointers in FFI code should simply be strings
who's value is a Pebble variable to be looked up. A simple helper was made to make conversion easier, being

	int FFIconvertValueToString (char *buff, unsigned long buffsize, FFIValue v);

where buff is a pointer to the buffer to write the string data to, buffsize is the size of the buffer, and v is
the value to convert. 

The FFI interface also provides a few more useful helpers, including but not limited to

	FFIValue FFIconvertValueToWord (FFIValue v, FFIvars *vars, FFIArena *persistAlloc); - convert an FFIValue
		of any type to an FFIValue of type word (unsigned long)
	void FFIstdoutPrint (char *msg); - print data to standard output with basic formatting such as newline 
		handling
	void FFIexit (int status); - error and abort the virtual machine
	
To see the full list, please view ffi/ffi.h

A simple example, take an argument and print it to standard output, may be something like


#include "ffi.h"
#include "limits.h"

	void escape_std_io_print (FFIvars *vars, unsigned long long seed, FFIArena *scratchAlloc,
		FFIArena *tempAlloc, FFIArena *persistAlloc, struct Limits *limits)
	{
		char *ptrbuff = FFIallocateMemory(tempAlloc, limits->instructions_varnamesize);
		char *msgbuff = FFIallocateMemory(tempAlloc, limits->instructions_varnamesize);

		FFIValue *ptr = FFIreadVariable(vars, "__Escape_std.io.print_ARG0");
		FFIconvertValueToString(ptrbuff, limits->instructions_varnamesize, *ptr);

		FFIValue *msg = FFIreadVariable(vars, ptrbuff);
		FFIconvertValueToString(msgbuff, limits->instructions_varnamesize, *msg);

		FFIstdoutPrint(msgbuff);
		FFIstdoutPrint("\n");
	}

which takes one argument and prints it to standard output.

FFI code should be compiled into the VM and cannot be removed after this. You can add your FFI code to the
VM by adding

	- a registry entry to ffi/escapes.h, which maps the Pebble sequence to the C function
	- an include line in ffi/sequences.h importing your FFI code
	- a .h file defining your FFI sequence in libs/(group)/(subgroup(s))/(name).h
	- a .c file defining the FFI code in platform/(platform)/(group)/(subgroup(s))/(name).c - it is usually
		fine to just put c as the platform
	- and an entry in build/build.c adding your FFI .c file to be built into the VM

eg. the previous example would add the line

	{ "std.io.print", escape_std_io_print }; - onto ffi/escapes.h into the escapes structure
	#include "libs/std/io/print.h" - onto ffi/sequences.h
	the C code into ffi/platform/c/std/io/print.c
	a .h file with the previously stated definition in ffi/libs/std/io/print.h
	"ffi/platform/c/std/io/print.c", - onto build/build.c into the ffi_c_srcs struct

And then just re-compile the build tool and the VM using that and your FFI sequence should be callable via
Pebble using the code

	New msg 'Hello, World!'
	New __Escape_std.io.print_ARG0 <msg>
	Escape std.io.print

and that should be all you need to get started writing FFI code! If you have any more questions, please look
over the source code. Also, it is important to note FFI code cannot assume a Libc is present, and should
instead use the VM provided wrappers in h/ - please read over those!	
